-- title:
--   Theorem 2 — target attainment in all four regimes (corrected: payment equality, monetary constraints, cost sign)
-- statement:
--   Consider the published model under Standing and the nonempty black-swan condition (8). Let r be either the exact rate vector r* from (7) or a vector generated by (23)–(25). Let b record whether the per-type monetary restrictions (15) are imposed. Write E_b(r,w) for global profit maximization on the corresponding feasible set. Then
--   $$E_b(r,w^*)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ (r=r^*\ \text{and}\ b=\mathrm{true}).$$
--   In the successful regime, all equilibrium qualities are characterized exactly:
--   $$r=r^*,\ b=\mathrm{true}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\bigl(E_b(r,w)\Longleftrightarrow w=w^*\bigr)\quad\text{for every }w.$$
--   Thus both necessary conditions, joint sufficiency, attainment and uniqueness are retained. With unrestricted cross subsidization the target is not an optimum for any source-generated rates; with the per-type restrictions a mismatched vector makes the target infeasible. The statement compares the four problems in the proof, rather than treating a constraint as an observed property of one unchanged problem.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: The source gives twice differentiable demands and costs on the quality box, patient counts and nonzero denominators implicitly. → The imported Standing context uses C² functions on the whole space, nonnegative service demands, positive target enrollment and positive target marginal service demand; finite types include Nonempty I.; reason: These are the published base conventions, reused without alteration; the stronger smoothness convention resolves endpoint derivatives and positive denominators resolve (3), (7) and the Bayes formula.
--   - reading: Theorem 2 says no cross subsidization in equilibrium and its proof compares (9), (29), (30). → A regime flag imposes the monetary equations after (15); otherwise feasibility is exactly the published box.; reason: The four-case proof on pp. 36–38 changes the optimization problem in Cases 3 and 4. Zero-enrollment types are handled by the monetary equations explicitly printed on p. 20.
--   - correction: The proof invokes noisy signals as if they always change the rates, and (29)–(30) print a conflicting cost sign. → Condition 1 is the exact equality r=M.rate, including noisy channels that preserve it; profit subtracts both cost terms.; reason: Theorem 2 condition 1 itself is (7). Equal accounting costs and a half-half channel refute the strict-payment-gap assertion; (10) and the expansion on p. 38 fix the sign. See MODERATION_NOTES.md.
-- source:
--   She, Ayer & Montanera, Can big data cure risk selection in healthcare capitation program? A game theoretical analysis, author manuscript (SSRN 3556992; MSOM 2022), pp. 20–21, Theorem 2; pp. 36–38, proof, (29)–(30)

import Definitions.Def_CapitationRisk_PerfectRA_Supplement_Signals
import Definitions.Def_CapitationRisk_PerfectRA_Supplement_NoCross

namespace CapitationRisk.PerfectRA.Supplement

theorem theorem_2 {S I : Type*} [Fintype S] [DecidableEq S] [Fintype I] [Nonempty I]
    (M : Model S I) (hM : M.Standing) (hB : M.BlackSwan)
    (r : I → ℝ) (hr : SourceRates M r) (impose : Bool) :
    (IsRegimeEquilibrium M r impose M.wstar ↔ r = M.rate ∧ impose = true) ∧
    (r = M.rate → impose = true →
      ∀ w, IsRegimeEquilibrium M r impose w ↔ w = M.wstar) := by sorry

end CapitationRisk.PerfectRA.Supplement
